<USER_REQUEST>
What type of carbonyl compound is primarily produced when Grignard reagents react with carbon dioxide (CO₂)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
When Grignard reagents react with carbon dioxide (CO₂), the primary product is a carboxylic acid. This is because the Grignard reagent acts as a nucleophile, attacking the electrophilic carbon of CO₂, forming a carbonyl anion intermediate. This intermediate is then protonated during the workup (usually with a dilute acid) to yield the carboxylic acid. 

The general reaction can be represented as:
\[ \text{RMgX} + \text{CO}_2 \rightarrow \text{RC(O)OMgX} \rightarrow \text{RCOOH} \]
where R is an alkyl or aryl group, MgX is the Grignard reagent, and RCOOMgX is the carboxylate salt intermediate.

Thus, the correct answer is $\boxed{\text{carboxylic acid}}$.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

