d. \( [ML^{-1}] \)
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The coefficient of friction is defined as the ratio of the gravitational force acting on an object to the normal force acting on the object. The normal force is the force perpendicular to the surface of contact.  

The force \( F \) is given by \( [M L T^{-2}] \), where \( M \) is mass, \( L \) is distance, and \( T \) is time.  
The normal force \( N \) is also a force, so it has the same dimensions as \( F \), which is \( [M L T^{-2}] \).  

The coefficient of friction \( \mu \) is given by:  
\[
\mu = \frac{F}{N}
\]  
Since \( F \) and \( N \) are forces, the coefficient of friction is a dimensionless quantity. This means it has no units and its dimensional representation is:  
\[ [M^0 L^0 T^0] \]  

Thus, the correct answer is:  
c. \( [M^0L^0T^0] \)
