","When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is added to a solution containing \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution, the presence of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) will cause the formation of metal sulfides. The solubility of these metal sulfides depends on their \( K_{sp} \) values.

1. **\( \text{Mn}^{2+} \):** Manganese(II) forms \( \text{MnS} \), which is insoluble in acidic solution.
   \[ \text{Mn}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{MnS} \quad (s) \]
   The \( K_{sp} \) of \( \text{MnS} \) is \( 2.6 \times 10^{-12} \).

2. **\( \text{Ni}^{2+} \):** Nickel(II) forms \( \text{NiS} \), which is insoluble in acidic solution.
   \[ \text{Ni}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{NiS} \quad (s) \]
   The \( K_{sp} \) of \( \text{NiS} \) is \( 3.0 \times 10^{-16} \).

3. **\( \text{Cu}^{2+} \):** Copper(II) forms \( \text{CuS} \), which is insoluble in acidic solution.
   \[ \text{Cu}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{CuS} \quad (s) \]
   The \( K_{sp} \) of \( \text{CuS} \) is \( 8.0 \times 10^{-11} \).

