","The organic carbon fraction (\(f_{oc}\)) is the primary factor controlling the transfer of \(^{137}\)Cs from soil to polluted rice. The transfer factor (TF) for \(^{137}\text{Cs}\) is often derived from the following equation:  
\[
\text{TF} = \frac{C_{\text{rice}}}{C_{\text{soil}}} = \frac{K_{137}^{1/2}}{\rho \cdot \frac{f_{oc}}{100}}
\]  
where \(C_{\text{Rice}}\) and \(C_{\text{Soil}}\) are the concentrations of \(^{130}\) in rice and soil, respectively, \(K_{137}\) is the uptake coefficient, \(\rho\) is the density of rice, and \(f_{oc}\) is the organic carbon fraction in the soil.  
The \(K_{137}\) value is typically approximately 4 x 10\(^3\). The density of rice (\(\rho\)) is approximately 1.3 g/cm\(^3\).  
Thus, the equation simplifies to:  
\[
\text{TF} = \frac{4 \times 10^3}{1.3 \times \frac{f_{oc}}{100}} = \frac{3076.9}{f_{oc}}
\]  
The transfer factor \(T_f\) is often expressed as \(T_f = \frac{C_{\text{rice}}}{C_{\text{soil}}\times f_{oc}}\).  
\[
T_f = \frac{K_{137}^{1/2}}{\rho} = \frac{4 \times 10^3}{1.3} = 3076.9
\]  
The average \(f_{oc}\) in soil is approximately 1.5%.  
\[
\text{TF} = \frac{3076.9}{1.5\%} = \frac{3076.9}{0.015} = 205,126.6
\]  
The average transfer factor is \(T_f = 3076.9\).",fluency,"# Task Description
