\[
\int_{-1}^{2} \left[(x^{2} + 3) - 1\right] \, dx = \int_{-1}^{2} \left(x^{2} + 2\right) \, dx
\]

The antiderivative of \(x^{2} + 2\) is \(\frac{x^{3}}{3} + 2x\). Now, we evaluate the antiderivative at the upper and lower limits of integration and find the difference:

\[
\left[\frac{x^{3}}{3} + 2x\right]_{ -1}^{2} = \left(\frac{2^{3}}{3} + 2(2)\right) - \left(\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3} + 2(-1)\right) = \left(\frac{8}{3} + 4\right) - \left(-\frac{1}{3} - 2\right)
\]

Simplify the terms:
\[
\left(\frac{8}{3} + 4\right) - \left(-\frac{1}{3} - 2\right) = \left(\frac{8}{3} + \frac{12}{3}\right) - \left(-\frac{1}{3} - \frac{6}{3}\right) = \frac{20}{3} - \left(-\frac{7}{3}\right) = \frac{20}{3} + \frac{7}{3} = \frac{27}{3} = 9
\]

