","The maximum frequency shift in the Compton scattering is given by:
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \lambda_{\text{scatter}} - \lambda_{\text{incident}} = \lambda_{\text{Compontes}}
\]
The Compton scattering formula is:
\[
\lambda_{\text{scatter}} = \lambda_{\text{incident}} \left( \frac{1}{1 - \frac{\hbar \omega}{m_e c^2}} \right)
\]
The maximum wavelength shift occurs when the scattering angle is \(\theta = 180^\circ\). Substituting \(\theta = 180^\circ\) into the Compton scattering formula:
\[
\lambda_{\text{scatter}} = \lambda_{\text{incident}} \left( \frac{1}{1 - \frac{\hbar \omega}{m_e c^2}} \right)
\]
For \(\theta = 180^\circ\), the angle between the incident and scattered photons is \(\theta = 180^\circ\), and the final velocity of the electron is in the opposite direction of the incident photon. The change in energy of the photon is equal to the kinetic energy of the electron:
\[
\Delta E = \hbar \omega_{\text{initial}} - \hbar \omega_{\text{final}} = \frac{1}{2} m_e v^2
\]
The maximum change in wavelength occurs when the electron is ejected with no kinetic energy, so the energy of the emitted photon is maximized, and the wavelength is minimized. This occurs when the scattered photon passes back through the same electron, losing all its energy to the electron.

The maximum wavelength shift is:
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \lambda_{\text{scatter}} - \lambda_{\text{incident}} = \lambda_{\text{incident}} \left( \frac{1}{1 - \frac{\hbar \omega}{m_e c^2}} - 1 \right)
\]
Since \(\hbar \omega = \frac{\hbar c}{\lambda}\), we can rewrite the formula as:
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \lambda_{\text{incident}} \left( \frac{1}{1 - \frac{\hbar c}{m_e c^2 \lambda_{\text{incident}}} - 1} \right) = \lambda_{\text{inside}} \left( \frac{1}{1 - \frac{\hbar c}{m_e c^2 \lambda_{\text{incident}}} - 1} \right)
\]
