","To solve this problem, we can use the principle of momentum conservation:  
\[
\boxed{\text{Total momentum before} = \text{Total momentum after}}
\]  
The total momentum is conserved, so the total momentum of the system (bullet + Earth) is zero before the bullet is fired (since both are initially at rest). After the bullet is fired, the momentum of the bullet is equal and opposite to the momentum of the Earth.  
\[
m_{\text{bullet}} v_{\text{bullet}} = - m_{\text{Earth}} v_{\text{Earth}}
\]  
Rearranging to solve for \( v_{\text{Earth}} \):  
\[
v_{\text{Earth}} = -\frac{m_{\text{Bullet}} v_{\text{bullet}}}{m_{\text{Earth}}}
\]  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
v_{\text{Earth}} = -\frac{(1 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s})}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}} = -1.51 \times 10^{-22} \text{ m/s}
\]  
The negative sign indicates that the Earth moves in the direction opposite to the bullet. The magnitude of the recoil velocity is:  
\[
v_{\text{Earth}} = 1.51 \times 10^{-22} \text{ m/s}
\]  

Thus, the approximate recoil velocity of the Earth is \(\boxed{1.51 \times 10^{-22} \text{ m/s}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
