\[
E = \sqrt{p^2 c^2 + m^2 c^4}
\]

where \(E\) is the energy, \(p\) is the momentum, \(m\) is the mass, and \(c\) is the speed of light.

Since the Σ⁺ decays very weakly (via the weak interaction), its decay product is a p meson and a π meson. The mass of the p meson is \(m_p = 938.3\) MeV and the mass of the π meson is \(m_\pi = 139.6\) MeV. The total mass of the particles in the decay is:

\[
m_{\text{decay}} = m_p + m_\pi = 938.3 + 139.6 = 1077.9 \text{ MeV}
\]

The energy conserved in the decay is:

\[
E_{\text{Σ}^+} = E_p + E_\pi
\]

The energy conservation implies:

\[
E_{\text{Σ}^+} = \sqrt{p_p^2 c^2 + m_p^2 c^4} + \sqrt{p_\pi^2 c^2 + m_\psi^2 c^4}
\]

The momentum conservation implies:

\[
\boxed{p_{\text{Σ}^+} = p_p + p_\pi}
\]

The original mass of the Σ⁺ is \(m_{\text{Σ}^+} = 1385\) MeV. The energy transfer in the decay is:

\[
\Delta E = m_{\text{Σ}^+} - m_{\text{decay}} = 1385 - 1077.9 = 307.1 \text{ MeV}
\]

This energy transfer is conserved in the form of kinetic energy of the p and π mesons.

The original energy \(E_{\text{Σ}^+} = 1385\) MeV is conserved in the decay. The energy of the p and π mesons are:

\[
E_p = \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{4p_p^2 c^2 + m_p^2 c^4}
